<article id="cache">
	<title string="Tables" idxkey="table" />
	<keywords>
		<keyword>cache</keyword>
		<keyword>rename</keyword>
		<keyword>table</keyword>
		<keyword>tabell</keyword>
		<keyword>swap</keyword>
		<keyword>desc</keyword>
		<keyword>lines</keyword>
		<keyword>nlines</keyword>
		<keyword>rows</keyword>
		<keyword>nrows</keyword>
		<keyword>cols</keyword>
		<keyword>ncols</keyword>
		<keyword>every</keyword>
		<keyword>range</keyword>
	</keywords>
	<contents>
		<syntax>
			{ANY} = TABLE(nRowIdx, colIdx=1, sheetIdx=1)
			{ANY} = TABLE(..., {"columnname"}, ...)
			{ANY} = TABLE(..., ..., {"sheetname"})
			TABLE(..., ..., ...) = {ANY}\n
			{STR} = TABLE(#, colIdx, sheetIdx=1)
			{STR} = TABLE(#, #, sheetIdx)
			TABLE(#, ..., ...) = {STR}
			TABLE(#, #, sheetIdx) = {STR}\n
			OTHERTAB() = TABLE()
			OTHERTAB(..., ..., ...) = TABLE(..., ..., ...)\n
			new TABLE(), ...
			remove TABLE(), ...\n
			clear TABLE()
			delete TABLE()\n
			show TABLE()\n
			save TABLE()
			save TABLE() -set file=NAME\n
			export TABLE()
			export TABLE() -set file=NAME\n
			rename TABLE(), NEWNAME()\n
			swap TABLE1(), TABLE2()
		</syntax>
		Tables are variables optimised for large datasets, particularly those organised in columns. They manage individual table sheets, each of which consists of one or more columns. Each sheet can have a different number of columns, each with its own data type, and each column can contain a different number of cells (without this affecting data access; see below). NumeRe thereby attempts to keep the memory usage for the data as low as possible.
		Thanks to this structure, tables can represent data with up to three associative dimensions without the need to expand dimensions across additional columns. It should be noted that tables <em>are not</em> matrices. Nor can they be fully embedded in arrays or other objects; however, their table contents, when extracted as arrays or sub-matrices, can be.
		By default, the table <code>table()</code> exists. However, using the command <code>new</code> (see below for further details), additional tables with different names can be created and removed again using <code>remove</code>:
		<codeblock>
            new TABLE1(), TABLE2(), ...
            remove TABLE1(), TABLE2(), ...
        </codeblock>
        <h>Data types in table columns</h>
		Columns in NumeRe tables can contain either numeric values, date-time values, durations, logical values, categories or <a href="nhlp://string?frame=new">strings</a>. If numeric values are written to string columns, they are automatically converted to strings. Writing strings to numeric columns, on the other hand, is <em>only</em> possible, if the strings can be interpreted as numerical values of said type.
		Time-date values are stored in seconds since Jan 1st, 1970 (UTC) with microsecond precision. Such values are generated and processed by special functions (such as <code>time()</code> or <code>timeformat()</code>). Time-date values can also be written as strings into the corresponding columns if they correspond to one of the following schemes or a date-time combination thereof (due to the lack of unambiguousness, the American format <code>MM/DD/YYYY</code> is not supported):
		<verbatim>
			DD.MM.YY(YY)
			YY(YY)-MM-DD
			YY(YY)/MM/DD
			DD/MM/YYYY or D/M/YY(YY)
			hh:mm
			hh:mm:ss
			hh:mm:ss.iii
		</verbatim>
		Values that describe a duration are also stored with microsecond precision; they can be generated using functions such as <code>weeks()</code> or <code>hours()</code>, or by subtracting two date-time values, and can also be specified as strings if they conform to the following format (parentheses enclose optional values):
		<verbatim>
            (-)(YYy )(WWwk )(Dd )(h)h:mm:ss(.uuuuuu)
            1y 2wk 6d 5:07:49.149080
        </verbatim>
		<h>Accessing data in tables</h>
        Elements of a table can be read and written using the following syntax:
        <codeblock>
            {ANY} = TABLE(nRowIdx, colIdx=1, sheetIdx=1)
			TABLE(nRowIdx, colIdx=1, sheetIdx=1) = {ANY}
        </codeblock>
        Sheets, columns and cells that did not previously exist return <code>void</code> when read. When written to, the necessary elements are automatically created and, where necessary, new memory is allocated.
		The following overview applies to the individual indices, whereby numerical indexing is carried out using natural numbers <em>greater</em> than zero (<expr>n = 1, 2, 3, ...</expr>):
        <list>
            <item node="nRowIdx">Must be a range, a numerical value or an array of numerical values. </item>
            <item node="colIdx">In addition to ranges, numeric values or arrays of such values, it can also use column names (or arrays of such names) <code>{"columnname", ...}</code> as indices. Automatically creates new columns with the column names used during write access if the columns do not already exist.</item>
			<item node="sheetIdx">In addition to ranges, numeric values or arrays of these, you can also use sheet names (or arrays of these) <code>{"sheetname", ...}</code> as indices. Automatically creates new sheets with the specified sheet names during write access if the sheets do not already exist.</item>
        </list>
		Ranges of elements can be defined using the syntax <code>FIRST:LAST</code> (similar to the <a href="nhlp://operator?frame=new">vector initialiser</a> <code>{A : B}</code>). If one of the two indices is omitted, it is automatically supplemented by the minimum or maximum index of that dimension, respectively. If both indices are omitted (<code>:</code>), the entire possible range is consequently selected.
        If an entire column is overwritten in the form <code>TABLE(:, j) = VALUES</code>, the type of column <code>j</code> can also be changed from numeric to string or vice versa. The column header is retained; any data previously contained in the column <code>j</code> is <em>completely</em> removed. However, a conversion can also be achieved using the table method <code>TABLE().convert(...)</code>.
		When accessing a table, the special variables <code>nrows</code> contain the number of rows, <code>ncols</code> the number of columns and <code>nsheets</code> the number of sheets in the current table, when used within its argument brackets (<code>nlines</code> is an alias). This also applies when other data objects are used in the expression or as an index set:<codeblock>TABLE1(:, ncols+1) = TABLE2(TABLE3(:nrows-10, 1), ncols-4)</codeblock>
		The column headings can be read and edited if <code>#</code> is used as an index for the row:
        <codeblock>
            {STR} = TABLE(#, colIdx, sheetIdx=1)
            TABLE(#, colIdx, sheetIdx=1) = {STR}
        </codeblock>
		If <code>#</code> is also used as an index for the columns, the sheet names of the table can be accessed:
        <codeblock>
            {STR} = TABLE(#, #, sheetIdx=1)
            TABLE(#, #, sheetIdx=1) = {STR}
        </codeblock>
		<h>Commands in combination with tables</h>
		Special commands are available in connection with tables:
		<list>
			<item node="new TABLE1(), TABLE2(), ...">Create the tables <code>TABLE1(), TABLE2(), ...</code></item>
			<item node="clear TABLE()">Delete the contents of the table <code>TABLE()</code></item>
			<item node="clear -memory">Delete all tables, <a href="nhlp://cluster?frame=new">clusters</a> and the automatic backup</item>
			<item node="show TABLE()">Display data from the corresponding table</item>
			<item node="showf TABLE()">Display complete record (only has influence if no external viewer is used)</item>
			<item node="rename TABLE(), NEWNAME()">Rename the table <code>TABLE</code> to <code>NEWNAME</code>. This cannot be applied to <code>table</code>!</item>
			<item node="swap TABLE1(), TABLE2()">Swap the contents of the table <code>TABLE1</code> with the contents of <code>TABLE2</code></item>
			<item node="save TABLE()">Save data from the selected table to a file with an automatically generated name. With <code>save TABLE() -set filename=NAME</code> the file name can be specified. File names with spaces must be enclosed by quotation marks</item>
			<item node="export TABLE()">Export data from the selected table to a text file with automatically generated file name. With <code>export TABLE() -set filename=NAME</code> the file name can be specified. File names with spaces must also be enclosed in quotation marks. See also <a href="nhlp://export?frame=new"><code>export</code></a> for further details</item>
			<item node="delete TABLE(..., ..., ...)">All entries in the selected range will be permanently deleted. Of course, it is also possible to select just a single element</item>
			<item node="remove TABLE1(), TABLE2(), ...">Removes the tables <code>TABLE1(), TABLE2(), ...</code> completely</item>
		</list>
		It is possible to append <code>-ignore</code> to <code>clear</code> and <code>delete</code> to suppress the required confirmation.
		<h>Methods for working with tables</h>
		In addition to a large set of dedicated table methods (such as <code>TABLE().convert(...)</code> or <code>TABLE().anovaof(...)</code>; details can be found in the symbol tree), tables can also be handled using the standard statistical methods from the vector methods section. For a specific application, these can be further refined using one or more modifiers:<codeblock>TABLE().METHOD.MODIFIER1.MODIFIER2</codeblock>The standard statistical methods are as follows:
		<codeblock>
			TABLE().avg
			TABLE().std
			TABLE().sum
			TABLE().prd
			TABLE().med
			TABLE().min
			TABLE().max
			TABLE().num
			TABLE().cnt
			TABLE().norm
			TABLE().exc
			TABLE().skw
			TABLE().stderr
			TABLE().rms
		</codeblock>
		They correspond to the standard functions of the same name and are executed on all elements of the selected table.
		The following modifiers are defined:
        <list>
            <item node=".rows">When used in combination with another method, this applies the method row by row and returns a corresponding number of results (<code>.lines</code> is an alias)</item>
			<item node=".cols">When used in combination with another method, applies it column by column and returns a corresponding number of results</item>
            <item node=".grid">Declares the current table as a data grid, so that the first two columns are ignored for the calculation</item>
            <item node=".cells(nFirst,nIncr=0)" >When used in combination with <code>.cols</code> or <code>.rows</code>, this selects the first (<code>nFirst</code>) row/column from which the multi-argument function is applied, thereby allowing the selection of table cells along the evaluation direction (e.g. with <code>.rows</code>, it selects the cells along the rows). Using <code>nIncr</code>, only every <code>nIncr</code>th column or row can be selected</item>
			<item node=".every(nFirst,nIncr=0)">Enables you to specify the first column/row (<code>nFirst</code>) and the increment <code>nIncr</code> using <code>rows</code> or <code>cols</code>.</item>
			<item node=".slices(nFirst,nIncr=0)">Selects the sheet <code>nFirst</code> to which the method is to be applied. Using <code>nIncr</code>, only every <code>nIncr</code>th sheet can be selected</item>
			<item node=".scwin(nCol,nWidth,nStep=1)">Defines a sliding window for the column <code>nCol</code> with a width of <code>nWidth</code> and a step size of <code>nStep</code>, within which a multi-argument function is applied to the data in <code>nCol</code>. For each step, the multi-argument function is applied once and the result is returned. The window is anchored to the left boundary of the interval, with only those steps being evaluated that lie entirely within the data range. The data range can also be specified using the <code>.cells()</code> modifier.</item>
			<item node=".srwin(nRow,nWidth,nStep=1)">Defines a sliding window for row <code>nRow</code> with a width of <code>nWidth</code> and a step size of <code>nStep</code>, within which a multi-argument function is applied to the data in <code>nRow</code>. For each step, the multi-argument function is applied once and the result is returned. The window is anchored to the left boundary of the interval, with only those values being evaluated that lie entirely within the data range. The data range can also be specified using the <code>.cells()</code> modifier.</item>
        </list>
		<example desc="Display data in the table <code>table()</code>, then sort columns 3-6 in ascending order by column 1 and calculate the sum of every second column:">
			show table()
			[...]
			sort table() cols=1[3:6]
			[...]
			table().sum.cols.every(2,2)
		</example>
		<em>See also</em> <a href="nhlp://new?frame=self">help new</a>, <a href="nhlp://remove?frame=self">help remove</a>, <a href="nhlp://datagrid?frame=self">help datagrid</a>, <a href="nhlp://save?frame=self">help save</a>, <a href="nhlp://string?frame=self">help string</a>, <a href="nhlp://cluster?frame=self">help cluster</a> and <a href="nhlp://delete?frame=self">help delete</a>
	</contents>
</article>